<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zongli Yamen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tsungli Yamen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zongli Yamen was the office in charge of foreign policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> in 1861 after the Convention of Peking. Before it, foreign relations had been handled by several agencies, among them the Ministry of Rites and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifan-yuan">Lifan Yuan</a>. It was supervised by five senior officials, at first all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, with Prince Gong as their leader in practice, and from 1875 it opened legations and consulates abroad. The Boxer Protocol of 1901 replaced it with a Foreign Office, the Waiwubu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen.jpg" alt="Zongli Yamen" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enrique Stanko Vráz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zongli_Yamen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
